TC9564 (QPS615) on Ubuntu 26.04 Resolute / RB3 Gen2
===================================================

Prerequisites
-------------

Use an ARM64 Resolute Qualcomm kernel with CONFIG_ARCH_QCOM and matching
headers. The boot image's DTB/FIT must contain the RB3 Gen2 staging
overlay. A qcs6490-rb3gen2-staging.dtso file in the kernel source is not
proof that it was compiled and included in the installed boot image.
Use the base-board configuration initially; the industrial mezzanine
has a separate overlay and is outside the initial test matrix.

Install matching headers from the same archive as the installed kernel,
along with efivar, ethtool, pciutils and iperf3 for setup/testing. For
archives with the standard Ubuntu header naming:

    sudo apt install "linux-headers-$(uname -r)" efivar ethtool pciutils iperf3
    sudo apt install ./tc9564-firmware_*_all.deb ./tc9564-dkms_*_arm64.deb
    dkms status
    modinfo -k "$(uname -r)" tc956x_pcie_eth

If that header package name is unavailable, obtain the image's exact
common and flavour-specific header packages from its kernel supplier.
Do not substitute generic or Yocto headers. Ensure the build link under
/lib/modules/<release>/build resolves to the prepared header tree.

If the earlier qps615-dkms and qps615-firmware test packages are
installed, purge them first. They ship the same firmware file and build
the same tc956x_pcie_eth module:

    sudo apt purge qps615-dkms qps615-firmware

The DKMS package name is tc9564, version 6.0.3+git2cbd7b9. The module name
is tc956x_pcie_eth. Its firmware is:

    /usr/lib/firmware/TC956X_Firmware_PCIeBridge.bin

On merged-/usr Ubuntu, this is also reachable through /lib/firmware.
The filename is case sensitive. Firmware is loaded into the bridge by
the driver; installing the package does not flash the board.

Activate the staging overlay (explicit user operation)
------------------------------------------------------

Run this on the RB3 Gen2 target, with console access available across
reboot. Staging is a board-wide overlay selection. Inspect its current
value first; if it already contains other selections, preserve them
using the board firmware's documented selection syntax rather than
blindly replacing them with staging.

    overlay_id=882f8c2b-9646-435f-8de5-f208ff80c1bd-VendorDtbOverlays
    sudo efivar -l
    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" -p

If present, export the original variable (including its attributes) to
a new backup file before changing it:

    overlay_backup="$HOME/VendorDtbOverlays-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).efivar"
    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" --export="$overlay_backup"

Record whether the variable was originally absent. A missing-variable
error is distinct from an unavailable EFI runtime or a permissions
error. Verify /sys/firmware/efi/efivars is mounted if efivar cannot list
variables. Keep the backup path for rollback; do not overwrite it.

To select staging, use a file with exactly seven bytes and no newline:

    overlay_payload=$(mktemp)
    printf %s staging > "$overlay_payload"
    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" -w -f "$overlay_payload"
    rm -- "$overlay_payload"
    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" -p
    sync
    sudo reboot

Only reboot after the write and read-back succeed. Package installation
does not execute these commands or make EFI changes.

After reboot
------------

    sudo modprobe tc956x_pcie_eth
    lspci -nnk
    ip -br link
    sudo journalctl -k -b | grep -Ei 'tc956|qps615|firmware|phy'

Expect the QPS615 Ethernet functions to bind to tc956x_pcie_eth and both
base-board Ethernet interfaces to appear. Interface names are assigned
by the system; do not assume eth0/eth1. Use ethtool -i <interface> to
associate each interface with its driver and PCI bus address, and
ethtool <interface> to inspect negotiated speed and link.

Confirm the live device tree includes the staging PHY supplies, reset
GPIOs and QPS615 GPIO controller. Successful EFI read-back alone does
not prove the firmware applied the overlay.

Troubleshooting
---------------

* Missing module: inspect dkms status and
  /var/lib/dkms/tc9564/6.0.3+git2cbd7b9/build/make.log. Verify matching
  headers and CONFIG_ARCH_QCOM. Retry with:
      sudo dkms autoinstall -k "$(uname -r)"
* Missing firmware: verify the exact file above and inspect kernel logs.
  This upstream revision does not declare MODULE_FIRMWARE, so modinfo -F
  firmware being empty is expected. Early network boot/initramfs loading
  is not validated by this package. If an image loads the module before
  the root filesystem is available, its initramfs must explicitly include
  the firmware; check with lsinitramfs. Normal tests load after root mount.
* Ports absent or PHY timeout: verify boot-image overlay inclusion,
  live-tree properties, board variant, PHY drivers and reset/supply logs.
* Secure Boot: use the distribution's DKMS module signing and MOK
  enrollment workflow if the kernel enforces signatures. A successfully
  built but untrusted module may fail to load with a key rejection.
* MAC addresses: the pinned driver falls back to random MAC addresses
  when platform addresses are absent. Record actual addresses during
  testing; persistence of a generated address is not guaranteed.

Rollback
--------

If the variable existed before activation, restore the saved export:

    sudo efivar --import="$overlay_backup"
    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" -p

If it was originally absent, delete only this variable instead:

    sudo efivar -n "$overlay_id" --delete

Verify the restored state, then sync and reboot explicitly. Restore only
when this will not discard overlay changes made since the backup.

To remove the software:

    sudo apt purge tc9564-dkms tc9564-firmware

Removal unregisters the DKMS module but does not unload an in-use network
driver or change the EFI variable. An already loaded module remains
active until explicitly unloaded or the system reboots. Do removal and
reboot tests over serial or another management interface.
